Let me look at the `fromISO` method more carefully. The key part:

```js
static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);

      if (start.isValid && end.isValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (start.isValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (end.isValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", ...);
  }
```

The issue: when `throwOnInvalid = true`, `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)` where `e` is a duration string like `PT5H30M` will throw `InvalidArgumentError: Invalid DateTime` instead of returning an invalid DateTime. So the code never reaches the `Duration.fromISO` path.

