## Root cause

`padStart(input, n)` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` zero-padded the **whole string representation including the leading minus sign**. For `year = -1`, `"-1".length (2) < 4`, so it computed `("0000" + "-1").slice(-4)` = `"00-1"`, producing `00-1-01-01`. Positive years were unaffected because they have no sign. This helper is what `Formatter.num()` / `PolyNumberFormatter` use for the `yyyy`/`yyyyyy`/`kkkk` tokens, so `toISODate`, `toISO`, `toISOWeekDate`, `toSQLDate` and `toFormat('yyyy')` were all mangled for BCE years.

## Change (only file I touched: `/luxon/src/impl/util.js`, function at line 101)

```diff
 export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
-  if (input.toString().length < n) {
-    return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
+  const isNeg = input < 0;
+  let padded;
+  if (isNeg) {
+    padded = "-" + ("" + -input).padStart(n, "0");
   } else {
-    return input.toString();
+    padded = ("" + input).padStart(n, "0");
   }
+  return padded;
 }
```

Negative values now pad the absolute value to `n` digits and re-attach the sign (`-1` → `-0001`, `-10` → `-0010`, `-100` → `-0100`, `-1000` → `-1000`, `-10000` → `-10000`). Positive values and the `n=0`/no-pad cases are unchanged. Other callers (`formatOffset`) only pass non-negative values (`Math.abs`), so they are unaffected. No tests were modified or added; no new features.

Note: the working tree already contained unrelated pre-existing modifications by someone else (`src/duration.js`, `src/interval.js`, and a second hunk in `src/impl/util.js` for `objToLocalTS`); I did not touch those.

## Verification

Baseline before fix: `npx jest` → **2 failed, 892 passed** (the known unrelated French `toFormat('FFF')`/`('FFFF')` failures in `test/datetime/toFormat.test.js`).
After fix: `npx jest` → **2 failed, 892 passed** — identical; no regressions. `npx jest test/datetime/format.test.js test/duration/format.test.js test/interval/format.test.js` → 93/93 pass (includes the existing `toISODate` ±YYYYY test). `npx eslint --quiet src/impl/util.js` → clean.

Standalone script `/tmp/verify_negative_years.js` (run with `npx babel-node --presets @babel/preset-env --only '/tmp/**,/luxon/src/**' /tmp/verify_negative_years.js`) — **ALL CHECKS PASSED**. It asserts, for years `-10000,-1000,-100, -10,-1,0,1,10,100,1000,10000`:
- `toISODate`: `-10000-01-01`, `-1000-01-01`, `-0100-01-01`, `-0010-01-01`, `-0001-01-01`, `0000-01-01`, `0001-01-01`, `0010-01-01`, `0100-01-01`, `1000-01-01`, `+10000-01-01`
- `toISO()` prefix equals `toISODate()`
- `toISOWeekDate` year (week-year) padded the same way (e.g. `-0002-W52-5` for year -1, since its ISO week-year is -2)
- `toFormat('yyyy')` and `toFormat('yyyyyy')` (e.g. `-0001`, `-000001`)
- Round-trip `DateTime.fromISO(dt.toISODate(), {zone:"utc"})` for years 0,1,10,100,1000,9999 → valid and year preserved. (Expanded years like `-0001`/`+10000` are not accepted by the ISO parser, whose regex only allows 4-digit or ±6-digit years — a pre-existing, out-of-scope parser limitation; the script reports this informationally.)

The exact bug-report cases now return `-0001-01-01` and `-0010-01-01`. No scripts were left in the repo (`/luxon/tmp_negcheck.js` was created for debugging and deleted; the `/tmp` scripts live outside the repo).

